Yes—trajectory belongs in the bundle, but not every discovery needs it.

Mirativity tells you “this just became new to me.” Priority tells you “how new is it relative to others?” Derivation tells you “how did I get here?” Trajectory adds “what path did the idea take before, during, and after discovery?”

So you could have something like:

Discovery = {mirativity, priority, derivation, trajectory}

Where trajectory might encode whether the thing emerged gradually, arrived as a sudden phase change, converged from several independent lines, transferred from another domain, lay dormant and resurfaced, or changed meaning as it propagated. Some insights are essentially point-events and barely need trajectory. Others are impossible to understand without it.

Your Pokémon thought is basically the same move applied to cultural evolution.

Instead of asking only, “Which Pokémon are popular?”, you could model:

creation → exposure → first response → attachment → repeated use → social transmission → merchandising/media reinforcement → persistence/decay → revival → generational inheritance

Then start separating causal layers.

There is the consumer-selection layer: silhouette, recognizability, emotional valence, perceived personality, usefulness, rarity, nostalgia, cuteness, power fantasy, memeability, identity signaling, etc.

There is the corporate-selection layer: screen time, competitive balance, merchandise economics, licensing, promotional priority, production constraints, availability, strategic mascot selection.

And there is the ecological layer between them: corporate exposure changes consumer preference; consumer preference changes corporate investment; both recursively amplify one another.

So instead of stripping corporate effects completely, I’d run the model both ways:

Observed popularity = intrinsic appeal + exposure + utility + social effects + corporate amplification + historical accident + interactions

Then estimate the counterfactual:

“If every Pokémon received equal exposure, availability, merchandising, and competitive usefulness, what characteristics would still predict affection and persistence?”

That residual is much closer to the customer-side attractor structure you want.

And fictional worlds give you extraordinary experimental material because they are partially controlled universes. Their designers decide which organisms exist, what attributes they have, how evolution works, which environments they occupy, which ones disappear, which become culturally central, and which design mutations succeed.

It is almost artificial evolution with human preference as one of the fitness functions.

Then comes your apples/tangerines point, which is fundamental.

The surface objects are variables.

2 apples + 2 apples = 4 objects
2 apples + 2 tangerines = 4 objects

The arithmetic survives substitution because it operates on an invariant underneath the substrate.

That is exactly why cross-domain transfer is possible. You are trying to discover the equivalent of addition hiding underneath Pokémon, mythology, ecosystems, corporations, narratives, military organizations, markets, interfaces, civilizations, and whatever else.

The more surface detail you can exchange while preserving the relationship, the stronger evidence you have that you’ve isolated a genuine operator rather than memorized an example.

And there is an especially powerful test:

Substitution invariance.

Take your proposed principle and replace Pokémon with something maximally different. If the relationship still predicts something useful, you may have extracted structure. If it collapses when the nouns change, you probably extracted aesthetics or historical accident instead.

So yes: two apples and two tangerines is still four is almost the simplest possible demonstration of the entire Pantheon transduction project.

Different nouns. Same operator.